@page "/getting-started"

<PageTitle>Getting Started · Blow</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Getting Started</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        One package, one namespace and one stylesheet - no services to register. Then a <code>BlowCanvas</code>
        in a container that has a height is a working node editor over your own <code>List&lt;BlowNode&gt;</code>
        and <code>List&lt;BlowEdge&gt;</code>.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Install</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The package runs on .NET 10 and 11, in Blazor WebAssembly, Blazor Server, a Blazor Web App and Blazor
        Hybrid. In a Web App it belongs in the project that holds the pages with a canvas - the <code>.Client</code>
        project, for pages rendered with <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code>.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">dotnet add package Bit.Blow</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Add the namespace to <code>_Imports.razor</code>. Everything in the library - the components, the model,
        layout, history and the clipboard - lives in it:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@ImportsCode" />
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        A canvas needs an interactive render mode: a statically rendered page shows its markup but never responds.
        WebAssembly is the one to choose for editing, since every pointer move of a drag is handled in C# without a
        network hop; <code>InteractiveAuto</code> works too, and so does Blazor Server, with each move a round trip
        over the circuit. On WebAssembly, outside AOT builds, the library can also be
        <a href="getting-started#lazy-loading">lazy loaded</a> - but not by a page with its own render mode, like the
        one below, as it stands: give <code>Routes</code> the render mode instead, or have the page load the assembly
        itself.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@RenderModeCode" />
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Link the stylesheet</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        From the host page - <code>Components/App.razor</code> in a Blazor Web App, <code>wwwroot/index.html</code>
        in a standalone WebAssembly or a Hybrid app. The link is required: the library does not inject its
        stylesheet, so leaving it out gives an unstyled canvas rather than an error. Put your own overrides after it.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@StylesheetCode" />
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>href="@@Assets["_content/Bit.Blow/bit-blow.css"]"</code> in <code>App.razor</code>
        serves the fingerprinted copy. The script module, <code>_content/Bit.Blow/bit-blow.js</code>, needs no tag:
        the component imports it the first time it needs it.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> A first flow</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The canvas fills its container (<code>width: 100%; height: 100%</code>), so the container needs a height -
        or give the canvas one directly with <code>Style="height: 600px"</code>. The graph is plain C#: the canvas
        edits the lists you hand it in place as the user works.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-demo blow-demo">
        <FirstFlow />
    </div>

    <CodeBlock Code="@FirstFlowCode" />

    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        That is a working editor. Nodes drag; dragging from a handle to another node connects them; clicking selects,
        <kbd>Shift</kbd>+click adds to the selection and <kbd>Shift</kbd>+drag on the background draws a selection
        box; <kbd>Delete</kbd> removes the selection; the wheel zooms toward the cursor, dragging the background pans
        and two fingers pinch. <kbd>Tab</kbd> reaches every node and edge, <kbd>Enter</kbd> selects, the arrow keys
        move the selection and <kbd>Escape</kbd> clears it.
    </p>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <strong>Add step</strong> goes through the canvas captured with <code>@@ref</code>: <code>AddNodes</code> puts
        a node in the list and <code>FitViewAsync(300)</code> eases the view over 300 milliseconds so the new step is
        in sight. Adding to <code>_nodes</code> directly works as well - the canvas re-reads the list each time your
        component renders.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Reacting to edits</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Your state is always the graph on screen. What the canvas raises tells you when something worth knowing
        has happened:
    </p>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li><code>NodesChanged</code> and <code>EdgesChanged</code> once an edit is complete - a drag released, a resize ended, a connection made, something deleted, pasted, aligned or nudged with the keyboard. Not on every pointer move.</li>
        <li><code>OnNodeDrag</code>, <code>OnNodeResize</code> and <code>OnMove</code> while a gesture runs, for live readouts.</li>
        <li><code>OnSelectionChange</code> when the selected set actually changes.</li>
        <li><code>OnConnect</code>, <code>OnReconnect</code> and <code>OnDelete</code> for the specific edits. A connection adds its own edge; <code>OnConnect</code> is for side effects.</li>
        <li><code>OnGraphChanged</code> once per completed edit of any kind, with what kind it was, who made it and the elements it touched - the one place to persist, validate or broadcast from.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>Nodes</code>/<code>NodesChanged</code> and <code>Edges</code>/<code>EdgesChanged</code> are bindable pairs, so
        <code>@@bind-Nodes="_nodes"</code> compiles - but the list instance never changes, so it is mostly a signal to persist.
    </p>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        When your own code changes the graph - adds a node, edits a label, flips <code>Hidden</code> - the usual Blazor
        rule applies: your component re-renders after its event handler and the canvas re-reads the lists. For a change
        made outside a render of your component - a timer, a message from a server - call
        <code>_flow.Refresh(remeasure: true)</code>, or <code>InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged)</code> on your component.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Calling the canvas from code</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Capture the canvas with <code>@@ref</code> for the imperative API - the counterpart of React Flow's
        <code>useReactFlow()</code>:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@FlowApiCode" />
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>OnInit</code> hands you the same reference once the canvas has rendered and measured for the first time.
        To drive a flow from a sidebar or a toolbar elsewhere on the page, pass the reference to them; nothing like
        React Flow's provider is needed (see <a href="examples/provider">Provider</a>).
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card" id="lazy-loading">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Lazy loading on WebAssembly</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Blow registers nothing, so nothing has to be taken out of <code>Program.cs</code>, and a WebAssembly app can
        fetch it only when someone opens a page with a canvas. Mark the assembly as lazy loaded in the project that
        runs in the browser, outside AOT builds, with a runtime switch next to the mark so the router knows the
        assembly is there to load, and load it before that page first renders:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@LazyProjectCode" />
    <CodeBlock Code="@LazyRouterCode" />
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The stylesheet is a plain <code>&lt;link&gt;</code>, served whether the assembly has loaded or not, and the
        canvas imports its script module the first time it renders.
    </p>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Only what renders after the assembly has loaded may use Blow's types - the pages it is loaded for and a layout only they use, not <code>App.razor</code>, <code>Routes.razor</code>, a layout every page shares or <code>Program.cs</code>. Those pages can hold the canvas and fields of Blow's types, structs such as <code>BlowViewport</code> included. The method that calls <code>LoadAssembliesAsync</code> cannot: WebAssembly resolves what a method refers to when the method first runs, which is before the load, and does not retry.</li>
        <li>No public type in an assembly the router reads in the browser - the app's own assembly first of all, the client project's in a Blazor Web App - may derive from or implement one of Blow's, close a generic type over one or constrain a type parameter to one: no public <code>IBlowEdgeRouter</code>, <code>IBlowLayout</code>, <code>BlowLayoutOptions</code> or <code>IBlowValidationRule</code>, no public <code>BlowNode</code> or <code>BlowEdge</code> subclass, no component that <code>@@inherits</code> one of Blow's and none with <code>@@typeparam TNode where TNode : BlowNode</code>. The router reads those types when it first builds its route table, after the first navigation's <code>OnNavigateAsync</code>, so such a type fails the app on its first page only when the app is opened away from a canvas: try it from a page without one. An <code>internal</code> class, or a <code>private</code> one nested in a page, is fine; a public one goes in a library of your own, lazy loaded together with Bit.Blow.</li>
        <li>In a Blazor Web App only the browser lazy loads, so give <code>Routes</code> a render mode in <code>App.razor</code> that brings the router - and its <code>OnNavigateAsync</code> - to the browser, as this demo's <code>&lt;Routes @@rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" /&gt;</code> does. Under <code>InteractiveAuto</code> the first visit runs over a server circuit and the browser downloads Blow on a later one.</li>
        <li>With per-page render modes the router renders statically on the server and never loads the assembly in the browser, so a page that renders a canvas fails there with <code>Could not load file or assembly 'Bit.Blow'</code> once it is interactive. Such a page loads Blow itself: it names none of Blow's types, awaits <code>LoadAssembliesAsync</code> in <code>OnInitializedAsync</code>, and only then renders a component of yours that holds the canvas.</li>
        <li>Every host other than standalone WebAssembly registers <code>LazyAssemblyLoader</code> itself when something it renders injects it - the server project, where <code>Routes.razor</code> runs while it is prerendered and over the circuit under <code>InteractiveAuto</code>, and a Hybrid host that shares the router's file: <code>builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;LazyAssemblyLoader&gt;()</code>. Outside the browser it downloads nothing, but loads each assembly the call names from the ones the app references and throws if one is missing.</li>
        <li>The condition leaves AOT builds out. AOT compiles every assembly into <code>dotnet.native.wasm</code>, so there is nothing left to download later, and lazy loading an AOT-compiled assembly can fail at startup. The switch is set only where the mark is, so an AOT build skips <code>LoadAssembliesAsync</code> as well: loading an assembly that is not marked throws.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Where to go next</h3>
    <ul class="bb-link-list">
        <li><a href="basic-flow">Basic Flow</a> - every default gesture on one canvas.</li>
        <li><a href="interactions">Interactions</a> - the options that change those gestures, with an event log of what the canvas raises.</li>
        <li><a href="custom-nodes">Custom Nodes</a> - your own node markup, with handles wherever you put them.</li>
        <li><a href="examples">All Examples</a> - more than eighty working pages, most of them ports of React Flow's own.</li>
        <li><a href="mcp-server">MCP server</a> - point an AI agent at this site and it reads the library instead of guessing.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

@code {
    // The snippets the page shows, coloured by CodeBlock.

    private const string ImportsCode = "@using Bit.Blow";

    private const string RenderModeCode = "@rendermode InteractiveWebAssembly";

    private const string StylesheetCode = """<link rel="stylesheet" href="_content/Bit.Blow/bit-blow.css" />""";

    // The live sample, as written: the component the page renders for it.
    private static readonly string FirstFlowCode = DemoSources.Components["FirstFlow.razor"];

    private const string FlowApiCode = """
        _flow.FitView();
        await _flow.SetCenterAsync(x: 400, y: 200, zoom: 1.5, durationMs: 300);
        BlowXYPosition point = _flow.ScreenToFlowPosition(e.ClientX, e.ClientY);

        _flow.AddNodes(node);
        await _flow.DeleteElementsAsync(nodes: [node]);
        BlowNode? found = _flow.GetNode("2");

        BlowSnapshot snapshot = _flow.ToObject();
        await _flow.RestoreAsync(snapshot);
        """;

    // The guide's own snippets, as written there: Bit.Blow.Tests.Mcp fails when they drift apart.
    private const string LazyProjectCode = """
        <!-- AOT builds are left out: see below. -->
        <ItemGroup Condition=" '$(RunAOTCompilation)' != 'true' ">
          <BlazorWebAssemblyLazyLoad Include="Bit.Blow.wasm" />
          <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="MyApp.LazyLoadsBlow" Value="true" Trim="true" />
        </ItemGroup>
        """;

    private const string LazyRouterCode = """
        @* App.razor - in a Blazor Web App, the client project's Routes.razor: the router waits for OnNavigateAsync
           before it renders the page. typeof(Program) is the project the router's file is in, in either kind of app. *@
        @using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Services
        @inject LazyAssemblyLoader LazyAssemblyLoader

        <Router AppAssembly="typeof(Program).Assembly" OnNavigateAsync="OnNavigateAsync">
            ...
        </Router>

        @code {
            // Set only where Bit.Blow.wasm is marked: loading an assembly that is not marked throws.
            private static readonly bool LazyLoadsBlow = AppContext.TryGetSwitch("MyApp.LazyLoadsBlow", out var on) && on;

            private async Task OnNavigateAsync(NavigationContext context)
            {
                // The pages with a canvas: fetch Blow before they render. A second call loads nothing again.
                if (LazyLoadsBlow && IsEditorPage(context.Path))
                    await LazyAssemblyLoader.LoadAssembliesAsync(["Bit.Blow.wasm"]);
            }

            // /workflows and the routes beneath it, such as /Workflows/42 (routes match regardless of case), but not
            // /workflows-archive. Add every other route that renders Blow. The path keeps any query and fragment.
            private static bool IsEditorPage(string path)
            {
                path = path.Split('?', '#')[0];
                return path.Equals("workflows", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
                    || path.StartsWith("workflows/", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
            }
        }
        """;
}
